     Kernel subsystems that wish to provide hhhhooookk points typically need to
     make four and possibly five key changes to their implementation:

     ••   Define a list of _h_h_o_o_k___i_d mappings in an appropriate subsystem
         header.

     ••   Register each hook point with the hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__rreeggiisstteerr() function dur‐
         ing initialisation of the subsystem.

     ••   Select or create a standardised data type to pass to hook functions
         as contextual data.

     ••   Add a call to HHHHOOOOKKSS__RRUUNN__IIFF() or HHHHOOOOKKSS__RRUUNN__IIFF__LLOOOOKKUUPP() at the point
         in the subsystem's code where the hook point should be executed.

     ••   If the subsystem can be dynamically added/removed at runtime, each
         hook point registered with the hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__rreeggiisstteerr() function when
         the subsystem was initialised needs to be deregistered with the
         hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__ddeerreeggiisstteerr() or hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__ddeerreeggiisstteerr__llooookkuupp() functions
         when the subsystem is being deinitialised prior to removal.

     The hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__rreeggiisstteerr() function registers a hook point with the hhhhooookk
     framework.  The _h_o_o_k___t_y_p_e argument defines the high level type for the
     hook point.  Valid types are defined in <_s_y_s_/_h_h_o_o_k_._h> and new types
     should be added as required.  The _h_o_o_k___i_d argument specifies a unique,
     subsystem specific identifier for the hook point.  The _h_h_h argument will,
     if not NULL, be used to store a reference to the _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_h_o_o_k___h_e_a_d cre‐
     ated as part of the registration process.  Subsystems will generally want
     to store a local copy of the _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_h_o_o_k___h_e_a_d so that they can use the
     HHHHOOOOKKSS__RRUUNN__IIFF() macro to instantiate hook points.  The HHOOK_WAITOK flag
     may be passed in via the _f_l_a_g_s argument if malloc(9) is allowed to sleep
     waiting for memory to become available.  If the hook point is within a
     virtualised subsystem (e.g. the network stack), the HHOOK_HEADISINVNET
     flag should be passed in via the _f_l_a_g_s argument so that the _s_t_r_u_c_t
     _h_h_o_o_k___h_e_a_d created during the registration process will be added to a
     virtualised list.

     The hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__ddeerreeggiisstteerr() function deregisters a previously registered
     hook point from the hhhhooookk framework.  The _h_h_h argument is the pointer to
     the _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_h_o_o_k___h_e_a_d returned by hhhhooooookk__hheeaadd__rreeggiisstteerr() when the hook
     point was registered.

     The hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__ddeerreeggiisstteerr__llooookkuupp() function can be used instead of
     hhhhooookk__hheeaadd__ddeerreeggiisstteerr() in situations where the caller does not have a
     cached copy of the _s_t_r_u_c_t _h_h_o_o_k___h_e_a_d and wants to deregister a hook point
     using the appropriate _h_o_o_k___t_y_p_e and _h_o_o_k___i_d identifiers instead.

     The hhhhooookk__rruunn__hhooookkss() function should normally not be called directly and
     should instead be called indirectly via the HHHHOOOOKKSS__RRUUNN__IIFF() macro.  How‐
     ever, there may be circumstances where it is preferable to call the func‐
     tion directly, and so it is documented here for completeness.  The _h_h_h
     argument references the hhhhooookk point to call all registered hook functions
     for.  The _c_t_x___d_a_t_a argument specifies a pointer to the contextual hook
     point data to pass into the hook functions.  The _h_o_s_d argument should be
     the pointer to the appropriate object's _s_t_r_u_c_t _o_s_d if the subsystem pro‐
     vides the ability for Khelp modules to associate per-object data.  Sub‐
     systems which do not should pass NULL.

     The HHHHOOOOKKSS__RRUUNN__IIFF() macro is the preferred way to implement hook points.
     It only calls the hhhhooookk__rruunn__hhooookkss() function if at least one hook func‐
     tion is registered for the hook point.  By checking for registered hook
     functions, the macro minimises the cost associated with adding hook
     points to frequently used code paths by reducing to a simple if test in
     the common case where no hook functions are registered.  The arguments
